# 4.5 Combinatorial Optimization

Many industrial problems require selecting the best combination from an enormous number of possibilities.

These are known as **Combinatorial Optimization Problems**.

For example,

suppose a delivery company has ten destinations.

The number of possible delivery routes exceeds

3.6 million.

With twenty destinations,

the number exceeds

2.4 × 10¹⁸.

Enumerating every possibility quickly becomes impossible.

Consequently,

companies rely on advanced optimization algorithms to identify high-quality solutions within practical time limits.

