# 4.6 Computational Complexity

Computational complexity measures how the required computational resources increase as the problem size grows.

Algorithms are commonly classified using **Big-O notation**.

Examples include

| Complexity | Growth |
|------------|---------|
| O(1) | Constant |
| O(log n) | Logarithmic |
| O(n) | Linear |
| O(n log n) | Efficient |
| O(n²) | Polynomial |
| O(2ⁿ) | Exponential |
| O(n!) | Factorial |

Optimization problems often exhibit exponential or factorial growth, making them extremely difficult to solve exactly.

